<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Joseph Goebbels</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1897">1897</a>–<a href="/years/1945">1945</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Joseph Goebbels was a Nazi politician and Hitler's Minister of Propaganda from 1933 to 1945. As <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauleiter">Gauleiter</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berlin">Berlin</a> and head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reich-chamber-of-culture">Reich Chamber of Culture</a>, he controlled the press, radio, film and arts, built up the cult of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuhrer">Führer</a> and stirred hatred of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. He proclaimed '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/total-war">total war</a>' in 1943, and on 1 May 1945, after poisoning their six children, he and his wife killed themselves in Berlin.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/joseph-goebbels.jpg" alt="Joseph Goebbels" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Heinrich Hoffmann · CC BY-SA 3.0 de · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bundesarchiv_Bild_146-1968-101-20A,_Joseph_Goebbels_(cropped)(b).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Joseph Goebbels</b>’s Ministry of Propaganda controlled press, radio, film and the arts through the Reich Chamber of Culture.</blockquote><a href="/empire/nazi-germany">Nazi Germany · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Joseph Goebbels</b>, the propaganda minister, valued simple popular songs as a tool, and between 1933 and 1945 some 15,000 Nazi musical works were produced, spread through the new mass media of film and radio.</blockquote><a href="/songs/erika">Erika</a></li><li><blockquote>Its words were written in 1929 by Horst Wessel, a Sturmführer of the party's paramilitary Sturmabteilung (SA, the &quot;Brownshirts&quot;) in the Friedrichshain district of Berlin, and were published in September 1929 in Der Angriff, the Berlin party newspaper owned and run by <b>Joseph Goebbels</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/horst-wessel-lied">Horst-Wessel-Lied</a></li></ul></section>
